    "created_at": "2026-07-08T12:48:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-07-08T12:48:56Z",
    "body": "Personally I think we should keep rejecting imports whose request returned a non-ok response. \n\nI feel there is a bit of a semantic difference between a request to an API endpoint that returns JSON (regardless of status), and a request resulting from a static JSON import. For these types of API endpoints, it is usually not necessarily expected that the result will be the same throughout the lifetime of a document. On the other hand, static JSON imports, much like JS imports (or text, CSS, etcetera), are (generally) only used to fetch static content. In other words, an API request returns data, whereas import statements import static files. I know this isn't technically true, and you can absolutely break this pattern if you wanted, in either direction, but this is my intuition.\n\nUltimately I think `fetch()` is more appropriate for requests whose contents are useful even if the status was non-ok. The use of `fetch()` is a signal that the server may respond with an error, that that is intentional, and that this is to be handled explicitly.\n\nI'm not opposed to extending the import syntax with `status: 'any'` to achieve the proposed behavior, but I would oppose intruducing it as the default.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
