    "created_at": "2026-07-08T16:04:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-07-08T16:05:25Z",
    "body": "I'm in the same boat with @vrugtehagel. From my point of view, `import` should ideally be meant for stuff that's \"always there\", and if for some reason a file is missing in the import tree, then it's ok for the program to throw instead of having gracious handlers around it like the body of the failed responses (and I say this as a public \"throw hater\"). If we think something might fail and we have to check, we can use `fetch` to have full control over the response, or we can use `import()` if we only want a simple `try`/`catch` if the import is gonna fail.\n\nMaybe its just me on this last thing, but I don't even think is great to add `status: 'any'` because it's adding even more complexity to `import` definitions that should ideally be simpler than that. It made sense to add support to `type` for other files that have to \"always be there\", but adding error request body parsing to every `import` feels to me like something we \"shouldn't need\".",
